RE-DIFF NOTE (7.14): ROCR-Runtime moved from its own standalone repo into
the ROCm/rocm-systems monorepo (projects/rocr-runtime) since 6.4.4, and
libhsakmt/src/fmm.c saw a real internal refactor alongside the move -- all
of gpu_mem[]/gpu_mem_count/svm, previously free-standing global/static
state, moved into a per-connection `struct hsa_kfd_fmm_context`, itself
reached via `HsaKFDContext *ctx` (ROCR-Runtime 7.x supports multiple
independent KFD connections in one process; 6.4.4 did not). Three real
changes were needed, confirmed by reading the actual current source, not
assumed -- the fields and functions the patch touches (`struct vm_object`,
`struct manageable_aperture`, `vm_create_and_init_object`,
`aperture_release_area`) are otherwise byte-for-byte identical to 6.4.4:

1. Every function this patch adds or edits gained an `HsaKFDContext *ctx`
   parameter, threaded through to `ctx->fd` -- the global `hsakmt_kfd_fd`
   6.4.4 called `hsakmt_ioctl()` against is gone entirely in 7.14 (0
   occurrences in the pinned source; `hsakmt_ioctl(int fd, ...)` now takes
   the fd explicitly). `__fmm_release()` itself already gained this `ctx`
   parameter upstream, independent of this patch -- the diff only adds the
   same parameter to the two new functions it introduces
   (`fmm_do_release`, `fmm_defer_release`) so they can reach `ctx->fd`.
   The deferred-release FIFO itself still lives on the aperture (as
   before), and an aperture is only ever reachable through the one
   HsaKFDContext that owns it (ROCm's own kfdcontext.h: "these contexts
   are fully isolated from one another"), so a parked entry is always
   flushed by a later call carrying the same ctx that queued it -- no
   cross-context leak.

2. `fmm_is_scratch_aperture()` -- used by the patch's scratch-bypass
   branch inside `__fmm_release()` to keep scratch-backed objects out of
   the defer FIFO (fmm_release_scratch()'s teardown loop requires
   immediate, not deferred, removal) -- changed both its signature and
   its return type: 6.4.4 was `fmm_is_scratch_aperture(const void
   *address)` returning `manageable_aperture_t *` (specifically
   `&gpu_mem[i].scratch_physical`, the same aperture object being
   released into, so `== aperture` was a valid identity check); 7.14 is
   `fmm_is_scratch_aperture(struct hsa_kfd_fmm_context *fmm_ctx, const
   void *address)` returning `gpu_mem_t *` (the whole per-GPU struct, a
   different type that can no longer be compared against `aperture`).
   Ported to a non-NULL check instead of an identity comparison -- safe
   because `object` was already found inside `aperture`'s own tree by
   the caller before `__fmm_release()` ever sees it, so a scratch hit at
   this address can only mean `aperture` itself is the scratch aperture.
   `hsakmt_fmm_release()` (the primary caller reached from
   `HSAKMT_STATUS hsakmt_fmm_release(HsaKFDContext *ctx, void *address)`)
   also special-cases scratch addresses before ever reaching
   `__fmm_release()`, exactly as in 6.4.4 -- this patch's own check is
   defense-in-depth for `__fmm_release()`'s other ten-plus call sites,
   same as on 6.4.4.

3. Pure line-number drift from unrelated upstream churn above each
   anchor (new VA-width comment/macros, a `bind_mem_to_numa` forward
   declaration, a `deferred/svm` struct reshuffle) -- no content changes
   needed beyond (1) and (2).

